+# Agentic MTTR, Overrides and Runbook
+
+Purpose
+- Define operator procedures for handling agent-driven incidents, ensure Mean Time To Recovery (MTTR) targets and manual override steps.
+
+MTTR Targets
+- Critical agent actions (impacting production traffic): MTTR <= 15 minutes (human escalation)
+- Medium-impact actions: MTTR <= 1 hour
+- Low-impact actions: MTTR <= 4 hours
+
+Automatic detection & circuit breaker
+- The agent circuit breaker will trip after N failures within a window (see services/agent/circuit_breaker.py).
+- When tripped:
+  - All automated agent executions are blocked.
+  - Notifications sent to SRE channel and incident created automatically.
+
+Manual override steps (SRE)
+1) Identify AgentAction CR name from alert or audit logs.
+2) Inspect evidence in S3 under `agent_audit/<action_id>/` using aws s3 ls/cp. Review the plan and per-step execution results.
+3) If safe rollback is required:
+   - If the AgentAction included a deployment image change, rollback via:
+       kubectl -n <ns> set image deployment/<deployment> <container>=<previous-image>
+   - If scale action caused instability, restore previous replica count recorded in AgentAction.status.
+4) Clear circuit breaker for namespace after root cause mitigated:
+   - Run administrative command: ./ops/scripts/clear_agent_circuit.sh <namespace>
+5) Postmortem:
+   - Add entry in incident tracker with links to S3 evidence and AgentAction CR YAML.
+   - Identify whether to update safe_policy.json, tool wrappers or planner prompts.
+
+SME & ML Lead responsibilities
+- Approve any policy changes recommended by agents before they are automatically allowed for medium/high risk actions.
+
+Audit & Evidence
+- All agent actions must be archived to evidence bucket with retention policy in place (Object Lock).
+ - Use services.agent.audit_logger to fetch or verify logs.
+
